content:
Node 22's global `fetch` is undici, and it does cover both cases you named — but not through the APIs node-fetch gave you, so it is not a drop-in.

### Streaming bodies
`res.body` is a WHATWG `ReadableStream`, not a Node `Readable`. It is async-iterable, and `Readable.fromWeb()` bridges it:

```javascript
const { Readable } = require("node:stream");
const { pipeline } = require("node:stream/promises");

const res = await fetch(url);
await pipeline(Readable.fromWeb(res.body), fs.createWriteStream("out.bin"));
```

Sending a stream *as* a request body additionally needs `duplex: "half"` in the init object — node-fetch never required that, and omitting it throws.

### Proxy
There is no `agent` option. undici uses dispatchers:

```javascript
const { ProxyAgent, setGlobalDispatcher } = require("undici");
setGlobalDispatcher(new ProxyAgent("http://proxy:3128"));
```

Node bundles undici internally but does not expose it as a requirable module, so you still need `npm i undici` for `ProxyAgent`. That is one dependency traded for another — the win is that undici is CJS-consumable.

### If you keep node-fetch
Your `await import()` failed only because top-level await does not exist in CJS. Move it into a function and cache the promise:

```javascript
let mod;
const getFetch = () => (mod ??= import("node-fetch").then((m) => m.default));

async function sync() {
  const fetch = await getFetch();
  const res = await fetch(url);
  // ...
}
```

Every entry point becomes async, which is the real cost. On Node 22.12 and later `require()` of an ESM module works without a flag (on your 22.11 it is behind `--experimental-require-module`), so a patch bump may make the original line work — but it still fails for any ESM graph containing top-level await, so do not build on it if you pin Node loosely.

The warning is not the bug, it is the smoke. You add one listener per request to an emitter that lives as long as the process and never remove it, so after 50k requests you have 50k closures, each pinning a `ServerResponse`, which pins its socket and any buffered request data. `bus.setMaxListeners(0)` would only silence the counter at 11 while the array keeps growing.

The listener has to come off whichever way the response ends. `res` emits `'close'` after the response has finished **and** when the connection is terminated early, so it is the single hook that covers normal end, error, and client abort:

```javascript
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  const onShutdown = () => res.end();
  bus.once("shutdown", onShutdown);
  res.on("close", () => bus.off("shutdown", onShutdown));

  res.end("ok");
});
```

`once` is not enough by itself: `shutdown` may never fire, and until it does the listener is retained. `'finish'` alone is not enough either — it does not fire on an aborted request, and aborted requests are exactly the traffic that accumulates.

If all you actually want is "end every in-flight response on shutdown", drop the emitter and hold the responses directly. The lifetime becomes obvious and you get a live count for free:

```javascript
const live = new Set();

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  live.add(res);
  res.on("close", () => live.delete(res));
  res.end("ok");
});

// on shutdown
for (const res of live) res.end();
```

For a real graceful shutdown, `server.close()` plus `server.closeIdleConnections()` (Node 18.2+) already drains idle sockets, and you only need the set for connections you want to cut short rather than wait out.

`write()` never refuses a chunk. The return value is advisory: `false` means "the internal buffer is at or past `highWaterMark`, please stop until `drain`", but the chunk is queued regardless. Your loop ignores it and never yields, so the event loop never turns, the fs write callbacks never run, `drain` is never emitted, and 5M strings pile up in `writableLength`. The stream is doing exactly what it documents — backpressure is only useful if someone reads the signal.

The pattern that reads it, with a generator so the backpressure handling is not yours to get wrong:

```javascript
const fs = require("node:fs");
const { Readable } = require("node:stream");
const { pipeline } = require("node:stream/promises");

function* rows() {
  for (let i = 0; i < 5_000_000; i++) yield JSON.stringify({ i }) + "\n";
}

await pipeline(Readable.from(rows()), fs.createWriteStream("big.ndjson"));
```

`pipeline` pauses the source when the destination returns `false`, resumes on `drain`, propagates errors, and destroys both sides — which the hand-rolled version usually forgets.

If you want to keep the explicit loop, this is the correct shape:

```javascript
const { once } = require("node:events");

for (let i = 0; i < 5_000_000; i++) {
  if (!out.write(JSON.stringify({ i }) + "\n")) await once(out, "drain");
}
out.end();
await once(out, "finish");
```

Two things worth knowing about that version. It is noticeably slower, because one `write()` per short line has real per-call overhead — batch ~1000 lines into a single string before writing and throughput improves several-fold, with memory still flat because the batch is bounded. And `out.end()` does not mean the bytes are on disk: await `'finish'` (or `'close'`) before the process exits, otherwise you truncate the tail — which is the other way this script silently loses data.

`ConcurrentModificationException` is badly named: it means "modified concurrently *with an active iterator*", not "from another thread". `ArrayList` keeps a `modCount` that every structural change increments; `ArrayList$Itr` snapshots it into `expectedModCount` when the iterator is created, and `next()` calls `checkForComodification()` to compare them. Your `names.remove(n)` bumps `modCount` without touching the iterator's copy, so the following `next()` throws. One thread is enough.

The second-to-last case is a genuine hole in that check. After the loop reads `"b"` the cursor is 2. `remove("b")` drops size to 2. `hasNext()` is implemented as `cursor != size` — not `cursor < size` — and it performs **no** comodification check. `2 != 2` is false, the loop exits normally, `next()` is never called again, and `"c"` is never visited. You get silent element skipping instead of an exception, which is why "no CME" must never be read as "the loop was correct".

Remove through the iterator, which keeps `expectedModCount` in sync:

```
Iterator<String> it = names.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    if (it.next().equals("a")) it.remove();
}
```

Or, better for this exact shape:

```
names.removeIf(n -> n.equals("a"));
```

`ArrayList.removeIf` bumps `modCount` once, compacts the survivors in a single pass instead of one `System.arraycopy` per removal, and cannot skip elements. It mutates in place, so it still needs the mutable copy you already build — `List.of(...)` is immutable and `removeIf` on it throws `UnsupportedOperationException`. If you would rather not mutate at all, `names.stream().filter(n -> !n.equals("a")).toList()` leaves the original untouched.

Two different things are happening, and the one your benchmark measures is not pinning.

Every task takes the same monitor and holds it for 50 ms. That serialises all 10,000 tasks: roughly 10,000 × 50 ms of wall time no matter what the scheduler does. A virtual thread that cannot acquire the lock does not run faster than a platform thread that cannot. Fixed pool, virtual threads, one thread — same number, because the critical section is the bottleneck. If your real workload has this shape, no threading model helps; shrink the critical section:

```
try (var exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) {
        exec.submit(() -> {
            Thread.sleep(50);                    // blocking part, unsynchronised
            synchronized (lock) { update(); }    // short part
            return null;
        });
    }
}
```

Pinning is real too, and your trace output is correct. On JDK 21 a virtual thread that blocks while inside a `synchronized` block or method pins its carrier and cannot unmount, so effective parallelism collapses to the carrier pool size — which defaults to `availableProcessors()`, hence "the same as a pool of 8".

It is version-specific. JDK 21 through 23 pin on monitors; JDK 24 removed that, so identical source scales there. Staying on 21:

- `ReentrantLock` is the supported answer. `lock()` parks the virtual thread properly and releases the carrier. Mutual-exclusion semantics are the same; you lose only the syntactic scoping, which `try { } finally { unlock(); }` restores.
- `-Djdk.virtualThreadScheduler.maxPoolSize=N` raises the carrier count and hides small amounts of pinning, but every carrier is a real platform thread, so it puts you back on a thread pool with extra indirection.

Worth checking as well: `synchronized` inside libraries you call (older JDBC drivers, some logging appenders) pins identically, and `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full` is how you find those — that is the case where you cannot just swap in a `ReentrantLock`.

It is one thread, and `.Result` is holding the exact thing the continuation needs.

Classic ASP.NET installs an `AspNetSynchronizationContext` for the request. It enforces one-thread-at-a-time access to the request context. When `await client.GetStringAsync(...)` suspends, the awaiter captures that context and schedules the rest of `GetJsonAsync` back onto it. Meanwhile the request thread is sitting inside `.Result`, blocked, holding the context. The continuation queues waiting for the context; the thread waits for the continuation to finish the task. Neither moves. Nothing threw, which is why your logs are empty and you only see a timeout.

A console app has no `SynchronizationContext` — `SynchronizationContext.Current` is `null`, so the continuation goes to the thread pool, a free thread runs it, `.Result` returns in milliseconds.

`ConfigureAwait(false)` fixes it by telling the awaiter not to capture the context, so the continuation runs on the pool and completes the task the blocked thread is waiting on.

But it is a mitigation, not the fix, and it only protects the awaits you control. Any `await` inside a library that *does* capture the context reintroduces the deadlock — which is precisely why "use `ConfigureAwait(false)` on every await in library code" is the standing guidance for anything targeting .NET Framework: a library cannot know what context its caller runs on. In your own application code, the real fix is to stop blocking:

```
public async Task<ActionResult> Index()
{
    var json = await GetJsonAsync();
    return Content(json);
}
```

Two other things while you are in this method. `using var client = new HttpClient()` per call exhausts sockets under load — hoist it to a `static readonly` field. And on ASP.NET Core there is no `SynchronizationContext`, so this specific deadlock cannot occur; blocking there is still wrong, but it starves the thread pool instead of hanging a single request, which is a much harder symptom to read.

A property getter returns a *value*, not a variable. `h.Value` produces a temporary copy of the struct; `Inc()` faithfully increments that temporary's `N`; the temporary is then discarded. Nothing wrote back, so `h.Value.N` is still 0.

The field version differs because a field access **is** a variable — it names a storage location — so `h.Value.Inc()` invokes `Inc` on a managed reference to the actual field and the increment lands. Array elements behave the same way: `arr[0].Inc()` mutates, because the array indexer yields a reference. `List<T>`'s indexer is a property, so `list[0]` is a copy — and `list[0].X = 5` is CS1612 precisely because assigning to a field of a discarded temporary is provably pointless. A *method call* on a temporary is not provably pointless: it may return a value, read state, or have side effects elsewhere. That asymmetry is the whole answer to "why is one an error and the other silent".

The language construct that turns this into a compile-time error is `readonly struct`:

```
readonly struct Counter
{
    public readonly int N;
    public Counter(int n) => N = n;
    public Counter Inc() => new Counter(N + 1);
}
```

`Inc` now cannot mutate at all, and the call site is forced to be honest:

```
h.Value = h.Value.Inc();
```

If you must keep a mutable struct, the explicit round trip is the only correct call site:

```
var c = h.Value;
c.Inc();
h.Value = c;
```

Worth knowing even before you go `readonly struct`: marking individual members `readonly` (`public readonly int Sum() => …`) makes the compiler warn wherever it has to take a defensive copy, which surfaces most of this family of bugs. The .NET analyzers CA1815 and CA1051 push in the same direction but will not flag your exact line — `readonly struct` is the guarantee, not a diagnostic.

This is deliberate, not a regression. `System.Text.Json` builds the contract from the **declared** type — the `TValue` of `Serialize<TValue>`, which inference here fixes to `Animal` — so it writes `Animal`'s properties. Newtonsoft resolves from `value.GetType()` instead. STJ's choice is defensive: if a `Dog` ends up in an `Animal`-typed field, you do not accidentally leak `Breed` (or a `PasswordHash` on some other derived type) into a public response.

`Serialize<object>(a)` emits both because `object` is special-cased: when the declared type is `object`, STJ falls back to the runtime type.

Three routes, with different output shapes:

**Runtime type, no discriminator** — top-level value only:

```
JsonSerializer.Serialize(a, a.GetType());
```

The catch is that it does not recurse. `Serialize(new List<Animal> { dog })` still writes each element by its declared element type, so this is not a general fix.

**`[JsonDerivedType]` without a discriminator.** The attribute has a type-only overload:

```
[JsonDerivedType(typeof(Dog))]
public class Animal { public string Name { get; set; } }
```

Now `Breed` is written everywhere `Animal` appears, nested included, and no `$type` is added. Deserialization cannot round-trip it — the reader has no way to know which subtype to construct.

**`[JsonDerivedType(typeof(Dog), "dog")]`** adds `"$type":"dog"` as the first property and makes deserialization work. That is a wire-format change; if a consumer validates against a schema, it is breaking.

For a type you do not control, the same configuration lives on the resolver rather than on the type:

```
var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
{
    TypeInfoResolver = new DefaultJsonTypeInfoResolver
    {
        Modifiers =
        {
            ti =>
            {
                if (ti.Type == typeof(Animal))
                    ti.PolymorphismOptions = new JsonPolymorphismOptions
                    {
                        DerivedTypes = { new JsonDerivedType(typeof(Dog)) }
                    };
            }
        }
    }
};
```

Same effect as the attribute, applied from outside, and passing `new JsonDerivedType(typeof(Dog))` without a discriminator string gives you the derived properties with no `$type`.

Yes, genuinely unspecified. The standard orders dynamic initialization within a single translation unit (declaration order) and says nothing about the order across translation units. `g_registry` in registry.cpp and `registered` in widget.cpp are different TUs, so a link order that runs the lambda first calls `operator[]` on a `std::map` whose constructor has not executed — hence the crash before `main()`.

Construct-on-first-use removes the ordering question:

```
// registry.h
std::map<std::string, Creator>& registry();

// registry.cpp
std::map<std::string, Creator>& registry() {
    static std::map<std::string, Creator> r;
    return r;
}

// widget.cpp
static const bool registered = [] {
    registry()["widget"] = &makeWidget;
    return true;
}();
```

A function-local static is initialized the first time control passes through its declaration, so by construction it exists before anyone can use it. Since C++11 that initialization is thread-safe (the compiler emits a guard variable), so your "nothing here is multithreaded" caveat is not load-bearing either way.

Two limits:

**Static libraries.** This is almost certainly the other half of your symptom. The linker only extracts an object file from a `.a` if an already-needed symbol resolves in it. Nothing references `widget.o` — the entire point of self-registration is that nothing does — so depending on where the archive sits on the link line, `widget.o` may never be linked in and the registration simply does not exist. Construct-on-first-use does not fix that. Use `-Wl,--whole-archive libwidgets.a -Wl,--no-whole-archive` (GNU ld / lld), `-force_load` with Apple's linker, a CMake `OBJECT` library, or one explicit reference per widget from a TU that is definitely linked.

**Shared libraries.** A single binary is fine. Across `.so` boundaries you need exactly one definition of `registry()` visible to everyone — put it in a shared library rather than a static one linked into several, or you end up with one map per copy and lookups that mysteriously miss.

The destruction-order counterpart bites the same way: the map is destroyed at exit in reverse construction order, and any other static's destructor touching it afterwards sees a dead object. If that is a risk, leak it deliberately — `static auto* r = new std::map<...>; return *r;` — with a comment explaining why.

The exact rules for `push_back`:

- If the new size exceeds capacity, the vector reallocates and **everything** is invalidated: all iterators (`begin()` and `end()` included), all pointers, all references to elements.
- If it does not reallocate, only the past-the-end iterator is invalidated. Existing element references and iterators stay valid.

That second clause is why caching `end()` outside the loop is a bug even without reallocation. Your loop re-reads `v.end()` each iteration, so that part is fine — what kills you is `++it` and `*it` on an `it` that still points into the old, freed buffer.

`reserve(64)` really is hiding the bug. It makes the loop defined *for this input*; the moment size crosses 64 you are back to undefined behaviour, now with the sanitizer quiet because you tested the small case. It is a bigger window, not a fix.

Index-based iteration is safe, because an index is not an iterator — it survives reallocation. You just have to decide what "end" means, or you get an infinite loop, since each append pushes `v.size()` out:

```
const std::size_t n = v.size();
for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    if (v[i] == 2) v.push_back(v[i] * 10);
}
```

Snapshotting `n` means "walk the original elements, append behind them". Re-reading `v.size()` each iteration means "keep walking until you stop appending" — legal, but non-terminating here.

One subtlety worth knowing: `v.push_back(v[i])` — passing a reference to an element of the same vector — is explicitly required to work; `push_back` must handle a reference into the container even when it reallocates. `v.push_back(v[i] * 10)` computes a prvalue first, so it never arises.

If the two phases are logically separate, the clearest version avoids the question entirely:

```
std::vector<int> extra;
for (int x : v) if (x == 2) extra.push_back(x * 10);
v.insert(v.end(), extra.begin(), extra.end());
```

One reallocation instead of N, and no invalidation to reason about.

Your reading is exactly right, and the mechanism is worth spelling out because it catches everyone who assumes `std::move` *does* something.

`std::move` is a cast, nothing more. `other.name_` has type `const Tracked` (the member is declared const), so `std::move(other.name_)` is a `const Tracked&&`. Overload resolution then considers `Tracked`'s constructors:

- `Tracked(Tracked&&)` — a non-const rvalue reference cannot bind to a const rvalue. Not viable.
- `Tracked(const Tracked&)` — a const lvalue reference binds to rvalues, const included. Viable.

So the copy constructor is selected, silently, with no diagnostic. `std::move` did its job; there was simply nothing it could move into.

There is no way to keep the member `const` and still move it. `const_cast`-ing the constness away and moving is not a workaround: `name_` was *declared* const, so modifying it is undefined behaviour however you obtained the reference, and this is one of the cases where compilers genuinely act on that assumption.

The idiomatic answer is to drop `const` from the member and take immutability from the interface instead:

```
class Wrapper {
    Tracked name_;
public:
    explicit Wrapper(Tracked n) : name_(std::move(n)) {}
    const Tracked& name() const noexcept { return name_; }
};
```

No setter, only a const accessor — callers cannot mutate it, and the compiler-generated move constructor and move assignment both work. Note that once you delete the hand-written move constructor entirely you also get the copy operations back and the class follows the rule of zero.

One consequence to go looking for elsewhere: a const member also suppresses the implicitly-generated *move assignment* and copy assignment, so `Wrapper` cannot be assigned at all, and a `std::vector<Wrapper>` copies every element on every reallocation instead of moving. If you have a class that inexplicably copies during vector growth, a const (or reference) member added at some point is the usual cause.

Adding to the mechanism answer: if the `const` is expressing a real invariant that you do not want to give up, move the constness into the payload so the *handle* stays movable.

```
class Wrapper {
    std::shared_ptr<const Tracked> name_;
public:
    explicit Wrapper(Tracked n)
        : name_(std::make_shared<const Tracked>(std::move(n))) {}

    const Tracked& name() const noexcept { return *name_; }
};
```

`Wrapper`'s move constructor now moves a `shared_ptr` — a pointer swap and no `Tracked` operations at all — so your instrumentation prints one move at construction and nothing afterwards. The `Tracked` object is `const` for its entire lifetime, enforced by the type, with no path to mutating it. The cost is one control-block allocation per `Wrapper` and an indirection on every read: a bad trade for a small string-like type, a good one for a heavy payload that several wrappers share. Use `std::unique_ptr<const Tracked>` when the value is never shared — same movability, no atomic refcount, but `Wrapper` becomes move-only.

What to avoid is trying to rescue the const member with a cleverer move constructor. There is nothing clever available: `Tracked(Tracked&&)` cannot bind a const rvalue, and the language has no "move out of a const object", because moving out is a modification.

Also, sanity-check the instrumentation before concluding anything from counts. Under C++17, `Wrapper w = makeWrapper();` involves no constructor call at all (guaranteed copy elision), and by-value `Tracked` arguments are constructed in place at the call site. A move count of 1 where you expected 2 is sometimes elision rather than a silent fallback. The way to tell them apart is that a fallback prints a **copy** — which is exactly what you observed, so in your case it really is the `const`.

The compiler is right, and `memcpy` is the sanctioned answer.

C11 6.5p7 (the effective type rule, informally strict aliasing) says an object's stored value may only be accessed through an lvalue of a compatible type, a qualified version of it, the corresponding signed/unsigned type, or a character type. `float` and `unsigned int` are none of those for each other. So the compiler is entitled to assume `*u = 7u` cannot affect the `float` object, keep `2.0f` live in a register, and fold the load. `-fno-strict-aliasing` just tells it to stop assuming.

The portable form:

```
#include <string.h>
#include <stdint.h>

static uint32_t float_bits(float f) {
    _Static_assert(sizeof(float) == sizeof(uint32_t), "size mismatch");
    uint32_t u;
    memcpy(&u, &f, sizeof u);
    return u;
}
```

This is not slow. GCC and Clang recognise a fixed-size `memcpy` between two register-width objects and emit a single `movd` at `-O1` and above. At `-O0` you get a real library call, which is why people benchmark it once, panic, and reach for a union.

`uint32_t` is a better choice than `unsigned int` here because it is exactly 32 bits by definition, so the static assert can only fail for a genuine layout reason.

The reverse direction has the same shape:

```
static float bits_to_float(uint32_t u) {
    float f;
    memcpy(&f, &u, sizeof f);
    return f;
}
```

Worth noting: your original function has a second problem independent of aliasing. `punned(&x, (unsigned int *)&x)` hands the compiler two pointers of different types that it is entitled to assume never alias, so even a `-fno-strict-aliasing` build is leaning on a codegen accident rather than on defined behaviour. Take one argument and return the bits, as above.

Regardless of technique, this all assumes IEEE-754 binary32 and a matching byte order. `__STDC_IEC_559__` tells you the first; nothing in C tells you the second, so if the bits ever leave the process, byte-swap explicitly.

On the union specifically, because the answer differs between C and C++ and that split is where the "widely tolerated" reputation comes from.

In **C** it is legal, not tolerated. C11 6.5.2.3p3 footnote 95 states outright that reading a member other than the one last stored reinterprets the object representation — the committee added that footnote precisely to settle this argument. So:

```
union fu { float f; uint32_t u; };

static uint32_t float_bits(float f) {
    union fu x;
    x.f = f;
    return x.u;          /* defined in C */
}
```

is well-defined C11. Two conditions attach. The members must not differ in a way that leaves you reading padding — here both are 4 bytes, so there is none. And if the bit pattern you read is a trap representation for the destination type, the value is unspecified: `uint32_t` has no trap representations, so float-to-uint32 is always fine, while arbitrary uint32-to-float can produce a signalling NaN — a value, not UB, but it can raise an FP exception once you compute with it.

In **C++** the identical code is undefined behaviour. No such footnote exists there; the union type-punning idiom works only because GCC and Clang document it as an extension. If the header is shared between C and C++ translation units, that matters. C++20's `std::bit_cast` is the `memcpy` equivalent and is `constexpr`.

Practical recommendation: use `memcpy`. It is defined in both languages, compiles to the same instruction, needs no type declaration to maintain, and does not invite the "just add another member" pattern where the padding and trap-representation caveats stop being theoretical. Keep the union only when you want an actual *storage* union — a tagged value that is sometimes a float and sometimes an integer — which is what unions are for.

One more escape hatch worth knowing: GCC and Clang support `__attribute__((may_alias))` on a typedef, which relaxes aliasing for that type only. It is non-standard, but it is far safer than a project-wide `-fno-strict-aliasing`, which disables the optimisation everywhere including in the code that was already correct.

Both instincts are right, and they are two separate bugs.

`realloc` may return a *different* block: it allocates, copies, frees the old one. Every pointer derived from the old block — `cursor` here — dangles the instant it returns. Reading through it, writing through it, even comparing it, is undefined. It "usually works" without ASan because glibc frequently grows small blocks in place, and even when it moves, the freed bytes stay mapped and readable. That is the worst possible failure mode: silent corruption surfacing in unrelated data much later.

The second bug is the assignment itself. `a = realloc(a, n)` overwrites your only handle with `NULL` on failure, while the old block is still allocated — a guaranteed leak on exactly the path where you can least afford one.

The idiom that fixes both is offsets plus a temporary:

```
size_t cursor_off = (size_t)(cursor - a);

int *tmp = realloc(a, 1024 * sizeof *a);
if (tmp == NULL) {
    free(a);              /* or keep a and propagate the error upward */
    return -1;
}
a = tmp;
cursor = a + cursor_off;
```

Offsets are the general rule: anything that must survive a resize is an index or a byte offset, never a pointer. That includes pointers you handed to *other* code — a callback that stashed `&a[3]` is broken by the same realloc, and no amount of local discipline saves you there. If a buffer's addresses escape, either stop reallocating it or hand out indices.

Two smaller points. `1024 * sizeof *a` can overflow on a 32-bit target once the count comes from input; compute in `size_t` and check, or use `reallocarray(a, n, sizeof *a)` on glibc/BSD, which does the multiply with overflow detection. And do not use `realloc(p, 0)` as a free: it was implementation-defined in C17 and C23 made it undefined, so `free(p)` is the only portable spelling.

The other way out is to not have a second pointer at all. The bug class is "two names for one buffer, one of which the allocator can invalidate" — offsets treat the symptom, a small owning struct removes the possibility:

```
struct ivec {
    int   *data;
    size_t len;
    size_t cap;
};

static int ivec_reserve(struct ivec *v, size_t want) {
    if (want <= v->cap) return 0;
    size_t cap = v->cap ? v->cap : 4;
    while (cap < want) {
        if (cap > SIZE_MAX / 2 / sizeof *v->data) return -1;  /* overflow guard */
        cap *= 2;
    }
    int *tmp = realloc(v->data, cap * sizeof *v->data);
    if (!tmp) return -1;                 /* v->data still valid, nothing lost */
    v->data = tmp;
    v->cap  = cap;
    return 0;
}
```

Callers address elements as `v.data[i]` at the point of use, so there is nothing to go stale between calls. The failure path leaves the vector intact and lets the caller decide whether it is fatal — unlike the free-and-return-(-1) shape, which forces that decision on behalf of code that may have wanted to retry with a smaller size.

Doubling matters too: it turns your growth from O(n) reallocations into amortised O(1), which is usually the real reason a "convenience pointer" appeared in the first place — reallocs were frequent enough that people started caching things across them.

Leave one runnable check behind, because the guard and the growth loop are the parts that break silently:

```
struct ivec v = {0};
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
    if (ivec_reserve(&v, (size_t)i + 1)) abort();
    v.data[v.len++] = i;
}
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) assert(v.data[i] == i);
free(v.data);
```

Run it under `-fsanitize=address,undefined`. Ten thousand elements forces enough reallocations that any stale pointer surviving in the code built on top of this gets caught here, rather than in production.

`sizeof(struct rec)` is 12 because the compiler pads so each member sits at its natural alignment: `char` at offset 0, three padding bytes, `int` at 4, `short` at 8, then two trailing bytes so the size is a multiple of the struct's 4-byte alignment. That layout is a property of your ABI, not of the file, and there is no reason the two would agree.

The portable answer is to read the record as bytes and decode fields explicitly — which is also the only version that handles byte order, something packing does not touch:

```
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

#define REC_SIZE 7

static void decode(const unsigned char *b, struct rec *r) {
    uint32_t v = (uint32_t)b[1]
               | (uint32_t)b[2] << 8
               | (uint32_t)b[3] << 16
               | (uint32_t)b[4] << 24;        /* file is little-endian */
    uint16_t f = (uint16_t)b[5] | (uint16_t)b[6] << 8;

    r->id = (char)b[0];
    memcpy(&r->value, &v, sizeof r->value);   /* two's-complement reinterpret */
    memcpy(&r->flag,  &f, sizeof r->flag);
}

unsigned char buf[REC_SIZE];
struct rec r;
while (fread(buf, REC_SIZE, 1, fp) == 1) {
    decode(buf, &r);
    printf("%d %d %d\n", r.id, r.value, r.flag);
}
```

The shifts are written against the *file's* byte order, so this reads identically on x86-64 and on a big-endian target — nothing here depends on the host. The `memcpy` into the signed members avoids the implementation-defined conversion a plain `(int)v` gives you when the high bit is set (C23 finally mandates two's complement; C11 does not).

Per-field `fread` also works and is portable in the same weak sense, but it does **not** solve endianness: `fread(&r.value, 4, 1, fp)` copies the file's bytes into an `int` using the *host's* interpretation, so it silently yields the wrong number on a big-endian reader. It is also four stdio calls per record instead of one, though buffering makes that cheaper than it looks. The byte-buffer version costs no more and is correct on both, so there is no reason to prefer it.

If you control which platforms this runs on, the packed struct is a legitimate shortcut, and the address caveat you read about is narrower than it sounds.

```
#include <stdint.h>

struct rec_packed {
    char    id;
    int32_t value;
    int16_t flag;
} __attribute__((packed));

_Static_assert(sizeof(struct rec_packed) == 7, "unexpected layout");
```

One `fread` per record, and the layout matches the file. What is actually forbidden is taking the *address* of a misaligned member and dereferencing it: `int32_t *p = &r.value;` yields a pointer that is not 4-byte aligned, and dereferencing it is undefined — it faults on strict-alignment targets (some ARM configurations, SPARC), and even on x86 a SIMD-widened copy through it can trap. GCC 9+ and Clang diagnose exactly this with `-Waddress-of-packed-member`, enabled under `-Wall`. Reading or writing the member *by value* (`r.value`, `printf("%d", r.value)`, `f(r.value)`) is fine: the compiler knows the alignment and emits byte-wise accesses.

So the rules are: never pass `&packed.member` anywhere, keep `-Waddress-of-packed-member` on and promote it to an error, and keep the `_Static_assert` so a compiler that ignores the attribute fails the build instead of misreading the file.

What packing does **not** buy you is byte order or integer width. Using `int32_t`/`int16_t` in the on-disk struct pins the width so the assert can only fail for a layout reason. Byte order has no fix at this layer at all: if the producer ever runs on a big-endian machine, or the file is transported, the packed struct reads garbage with no diagnostic whatsoever.

Practical split: packed struct when the file is a cache you produced yourself on the same ABI, byte-buffer decoding when it comes from another tool or another machine. Yours is the second case, so treat the packed version as the fast local experiment, not the shipping code.

If you need the pragmatic repair now, do it — but not with the conversion you proposed, on two counts.

**`ISO-8859-1` is the wrong source charset.** MySQL's `latin1` is not ISO-8859-1; it is cp1252. They differ exactly in the 0x80–0x9F range, which is where the interesting characters live: 0x92 is a right single quotation mark in cp1252 and an unassigned C1 control character in ISO-8859-1. Converting from `ISO-8859-1` turns curly apostrophes and en-dashes into invisible control characters — the same class of damage you were trying to avoid.

**Converting unconditionally destroys the good rows.** `mb_convert_encoding` never fails; it will happily take a row that is already valid UTF-8 and produce double-encoded garbage (`é` becomes `Ã©`). Since only *some* of your rows are bad, an unconditional sweep breaks the rest.

Guard it:

```
function fixEncoding(mixed $v): mixed
{
    if (!is_string($v)) {
        return $v;
    }
    return mb_check_encoding($v, 'UTF-8')
        ? $v
        : mb_convert_encoding($v, 'UTF-8', 'Windows-1252');
}

$rows = array_map(
    static fn(array $row) => array_map(fixEncoding(...), $row),
    $rows
);

$json = json_encode($rows, JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR);
```

`mb_check_encoding` is the discriminator that makes this safe to run over mixed data: valid UTF-8 passes through byte-identical, and only the genuinely-latin1 rows get converted. `Windows-1252` decodes every byte 0x00–0xFF, so it cannot fail either — which means this is a heuristic, not a proof. A row that is valid UTF-8 *and* was meant to be cp1252 will pass through unconverted; in practice that is vanishingly rare for names, because most cp1252 byte sequences are not valid UTF-8.

Treat this as a bridge. Log how many rows it converts, and when the count goes to zero after the schema is fixed, delete the function. Leaving it in permanently means a future encoding bug never surfaces — it just gets quietly guessed at.

The smallest correct fix is to cast the value, and it works with emulation left on:

```
$limit = (int) ($_GET['limit'] ?? 20);
$limit = max(1, min(100, $limit));

$stmt = $pdo->prepare('SELECT id FROM posts ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT :limit');
$stmt->bindValue(':limit', $limit, PDO::PARAM_INT);
$stmt->execute();
```

With emulated prepares — the pdo_mysql default — PDO does not send parameters to the server separately. It builds the final SQL string itself and quotes each value, and the quoting decision follows the *PHP value's* type, not just the `PDO::PARAM_*` hint. `"10"` is a PHP string, so it is quoted, and `LIMIT '10'` is a syntax error because MySQL's `LIMIT` clause accepts only an unsigned integer literal (or a placeholder), never a string.

Two things follow from that.

The cast is not cosmetic — it is also the validation. `$_GET['limit']` is attacker-controlled, and `(int)` collapses anything to a number, but `(int) "999999999"` is still a valid integer that will happily ask MySQL to sort and return a million rows. Clamp it, as above. `filter_var($_GET['limit'], FILTER_VALIDATE_INT, ['options' => ['min_range' => 1, 'max_range' => 100]])` is the more explicit spelling if you want a rejection rather than a clamp.

The same applies to `OFFSET`, and to any other clause where MySQL requires a literal — you cannot bind a table name, a column name, or an `ORDER BY` direction at all. Those must come from a whitelist in PHP:

```
$dir = ($_GET['dir'] ?? 'desc') === 'asc' ? 'ASC' : 'DESC';
$sql = "SELECT id FROM posts ORDER BY id $dir LIMIT :limit";
```

That is not string-building SQL from input; it is choosing between two constants. The distinction is the one that matters for injection.

There is a spelling of this that has no closure to weaken, which is usually the better answer for "publisher writes into a property":

```
final class FeedViewModel: ObservableObject {
    @Published var items: [Item] = []
    private let service: FeedService

    func load() {
        service.publisher()
            .replaceError(with: [])
            .receive(on: DispatchQueue.main)
            .assign(to: &$items)
    }

    deinit { print("deinit") }
}
```

`assign(to: &$published)` — note the inout projected value, not `assign(to:on:)` — is designed for this. It attaches the upstream directly to the `@Published` property's internal subject, manages the subscription's lifetime through the publisher itself, and returns nothing, so there is no `AnyCancellable` to store and no closure to capture `self`. When the view model deallocates, the `@Published` storage goes with it and the subscription is torn down.

Two requirements it imposes. The upstream `Failure` must be `Never`, hence the `replaceError`/`catch`. And the target must be a `@Published` property on the same object, so it does not generalise to "run arbitrary code on each value" — for that you are back to `sink` with `[weak self]`.

The trap to avoid is the sibling API: `assign(to: \.items, on: self)` **retains `self` strongly** and, combined with `store(in: &cancellables)`, produces the identical cycle you already have — with no closure in sight, which makes it harder to spot in review. If you see `assign(to:on:)` with `self` as the object anywhere in the codebase, it is the same leak.

If you are willing to move off Combine for this, the structured-concurrency version has no cycle to reason about at all:

```
private var task: Task<Void, Never>?

func load() {
    task = Task { [weak self] in
        for await items in service.stream() {
            self?.items = items
        }
    }
}

deinit { task?.cancel() }
```

Here the escape is explicit and cancellation is a first-class operation rather than a side effect of deallocation.

Start with the diagnosis, because `Task.detached` is doing more damage than the `Sendable` error suggests.

`Task.detached` deliberately opts out of the enclosing actor isolation, priority, and task-local values. That is why the closure is `@Sendable` and why `cache` has to cross an isolation boundary at all. If `warm` already runs somewhere with isolation — the main actor, or an actor of your own — a plain `Task { }` inherits that isolation, the closure is not `@Sendable`, and the error disappears without any change to `Cache`:

```
@MainActor
func warm(_ cache: Cache) {
    Task { cache.storage["key"] = Data() }   // stays on the main actor
}
```

Nothing crossed a boundary, so nothing needs to be `Sendable`. Check whether you wanted `detached` at all — most uses of it in migrated Swift 5 code are there because someone wanted "run this in the background", which is not what it means.

If the answer is genuinely "many isolation domains touch this cache", `@MainActor` on the class is the cheapest honest option:

```
@MainActor
final class Cache {
    var storage: [String: Data] = [:]
}
```

A `@MainActor` type is implicitly `Sendable`, so it can be captured in `@Sendable` closures freely. Callers already on the main actor keep synchronous access with no source change — the `await` ripple you are worried about appears only for callers in other isolation domains, and there it is correct: they really are hopping. The cost is that every access serialises onto the main thread. Fine for a cache read on a UI path; wrong for something touched by background parsing, where you will be hopping to the main actor thousands of times to read a dictionary.

So the decision is: is this cache UI-adjacent (use `@MainActor`), or is it infrastructure touched from everywhere (use a lock — see the other answer)? `@unchecked Sendable` on the class as it stands is neither; it is a promise you have not kept, and the checker will believe you.

The middle option you are looking for is a lock, and Swift 6 ships one that satisfies the checker honestly.

```
import Synchronization

final class Cache: Sendable {
    private let storage = Mutex<[String: Data]>([:])

    func set(_ value: Data, forKey key: String) {
        storage.withLock { $0[key] = value }
    }

    func value(forKey key: String) -> Data? {
        storage.withLock { $0[key] }
    }
}
```

`Mutex` is `Sendable` when its value is, so `Cache` conforms to `Sendable` outright — plain `Sendable`, not `@unchecked` — and the compiler verifies it rather than taking your word for it. Crucially the API stays **synchronous**: `warm` compiles unchanged, and so does every existing call site. No `await`, no ripple.

```
func warm(_ cache: Cache) {
    Task.detached { cache.set(Data(), forKey: "key") }
}
```

Two things this forces on you, both of them improvements. The storage has to become `private` — you cannot expose a mutable `var storage` and still be `Sendable`, so the dictionary is now reachable only through methods that hold the lock. And compound operations have to be expressed as a single `withLock` call rather than a read followed by a write, which is the race the old code had anyway:

```
func value(forKey key: String, orInsert make: () -> Data) -> Data {
    storage.withLock { dict in
        if let existing = dict[key] { return existing }
        let made = make()
        dict[key] = made
        return made
    }
}
```

Deployment caveat: `Synchronization.Mutex` requires macOS 15 / iOS 18. Below that, wrap `NSLock` in a `final class ... : @unchecked Sendable` — and there the `@unchecked` is *earned*, because every path to the storage goes through the lock, which is exactly the claim the annotation makes. That is the difference between the `@unchecked Sendable` you rejected (a bare class with a public mutable property, where the claim is false) and this one.

Do not call anything that can suspend or re-enter inside `withLock`. `Mutex` is not reentrant, and holding a lock across an `await` is not even expressible here, which is a feature.

`.iso8601` is a fixed configuration, not a permissive parser. It maps to an `ISO8601DateFormatter` with `formatOptions = .withInternetDateTime`, which expands to `[.withFullDate, .withDashSeparatorInDate, .withTime, .withColonSeparatorInTime, .withTimeZone]`. `.withFractionalSeconds` is deliberately not in that set, and the formatter is strict — anything the option set does not describe is rejected outright. That is the whole reason dropping `.482` works.

Adding `.withFractionalSeconds` to a formatter of your own fixes your payload but inverts the problem: that formatter then *requires* fractional seconds and rejects `2026-03-11T09:41:12Z`. Real backends emit both, often from different endpoints, so the two-formatter `.custom` strategy is the usual answer:

```
private let iso8601WithFraction: ISO8601DateFormatter = {
    let f = ISO8601DateFormatter()
    f.formatOptions = [.withInternetDateTime, .withFractionalSeconds]
    return f
}()

private let iso8601Plain: ISO8601DateFormatter = {
    let f = ISO8601DateFormatter()
    f.formatOptions = [.withInternetDateTime]
    return f
}()

decoder.dateDecodingStrategy = .custom { decoder in
    let container = try decoder.singleValueContainer()
    let string = try container.decode(String.self)

    if let date = iso8601WithFraction.date(from: string)
                ?? iso8601Plain.date(from: string) {
        return date
    }

    throw DecodingError.dataCorruptedError(
        in: container,
        debugDescription: "Not an ISO8601 date: \(string)"
    )
}
```

Two details that matter in practice. Hoist the formatters out of the closure, as above — constructing an `ISO8601DateFormatter` per value is expensive and is the usual reason decoding a large array is unexpectedly slow. And build the error with `DecodingError.dataCorruptedError(in:debugDescription:)` including the offending string: the built-in strategy's message tells you nothing about what it actually received, which is why you had to bisect by hand to find `.482`.

Order matters slightly: try the fractional formatter first. A string with fractional seconds fails the plain formatter, and a string without fails the fractional one, so either order is correct — but putting the common case first saves a parse attempt per value.

Catching `Exception` around a suspending call is not *always* wrong, but this specific shape is, and the mechanism explains why `cancelAndJoin` hangs.

`CancellationException` is a subclass of `IllegalStateException`, so it is an `Exception` and your catch swallows it. Cancellation in kotlinx.coroutines works by putting the `Job` into a cancelling state and having every suspension point throw `CancellationException` — so `delay(100)` throws immediately, forever, on every iteration. You log it, loop, hit `delay` again, throw again. The job never reaches a completed state, and `cancelAndJoin` waits for exactly that. Hence the log spam and the hang.

The idiomatic fix is one line inside the existing catch:

```
val job = scope.launch {
    while (true) {
        try {
            delay(100)
            poll()
        } catch (e: Exception) {
            currentCoroutineContext().ensureActive()
            log.warn("poll failed", e)
        }
    }
}
```

`ensureActive()` rethrows the job's cancellation cause if the coroutine has been cancelled, and does nothing otherwise. It covers a case that an explicit `catch (e: CancellationException) { throw e }` misses: an exception that is *not* a `CancellationException` but arrives while the job is already cancelling — a socket error caused by the underlying resource being torn down, for example. Rethrowing on the type alone would log that and loop; `ensureActive` exits.

If you prefer the explicit form, the order is load-bearing — the narrower type must come first, or the `Exception` clause shadows it:

```
} catch (e: CancellationException) {
    throw e
} catch (e: Exception) {
    log.warn("poll failed", e)
}
```

`while (isActive)` does terminate the loop, but treat it as a second line of defence rather than the fix: it only helps at the top of the loop, and any `try/catch (Exception)` inside `poll()` reproduces the same bug one level down where `isActive` cannot see it.

On "catch only what `poll()` can throw" — yes, and it is the rule that generalises past this bug.

The problem is not the `try` block, it is the width of the catch. Cancellation is signalled by an exception, so any handler broad enough to include `CancellationException` is a handler that can swallow cancellation. Narrow it to what the call actually fails with:

```
try {
    poll()
} catch (e: IOException) {
    log.warn("poll failed", e)
} catch (e: HttpException) {
    log.warn("poll rejected: ${e.code}", e)
}
```

`CancellationException` is none of those, so it propagates and the coroutine unwinds correctly. No rethrow clause to remember, and the code documents what can actually go wrong.

Two related traps worth grepping for in the same codebase:

**`runCatching`** catches `Throwable`. It has the identical problem in a much shorter spelling, and it is used everywhere precisely because it looks harmless:

```
val result = runCatching { poll() }   // swallows cancellation
```

There is no cancellation-aware version in the standard library; write one:

```
suspend inline fun <T> runCatchingCancellable(block: () -> T): Result<T> =
    try {
        Result.success(block())
    } catch (e: CancellationException) {
        throw e
    } catch (e: Throwable) {
        Result.failure(e)
    }
```

**`catch (e: Throwable)` in a generic retry or error-reporting helper.** Same issue, and worse, because a helper is applied to many call sites at once.

The edge case none of this covers: cleanup. Once a coroutine is cancelled, every suspending call in its `finally` block throws immediately, so you cannot `await` a close or flush on the way out. That is what `NonCancellable` is for:

```
try {
    poll()
} finally {
    withContext(NonCancellable) { connection.closeGracefully() }
}
```

Use it only for short, bounded cleanup — it is an explicit opt-out of cancellation, and a slow operation inside it makes `cancelAndJoin` hang for a different reason than the one you started with.

Third option: restructure so there is no `try/catch` spanning a cancellation point at all. `Flow.retry` is already cancellation-aware, so the correctness is in the operator rather than in a rule you have to remember at every call site.

```
val job = scope.launch {
    flow {
        while (true) {
            emit(poll())
            delay(100)
        }
    }
    .retry { cause ->
        log.warn("poll failed", cause)
        delay(100)          // backoff before the retry
        true
    }
    .collect { handleResult(it) }
}
```

`retry` and `retryWhen` do not catch `CancellationException` — the predicate is never invoked for it, and cancellation propagates out of `collect`. So `job.cancelAndJoin()` completes even though there is no `isActive` check, no rethrow, and no explicit exception type anywhere. The property you want is structural rather than conventional.

What this buys beyond correctness: the retry policy is now a value you can vary. Cap the attempts (`retry(3) { … }`), add exponential backoff in the predicate, or stop retrying on non-recoverable causes:

```
.retry { cause -> cause is IOException }
```

What it costs: a `Flow` where you had a loop, and a slightly indirect control flow — the `delay(100)` between polls and the `delay(100)` before a retry are now in two different places and it is easy to end up with one of them missing. It is also more machinery than the problem strictly needs if this is the only polling loop in the codebase.

Where it pays off is when there are several. A `retry` policy composes and can be extracted (`fun <T> Flow<T>.withPollingRetry(): Flow<T>`), whereas the "remember to call `ensureActive()` in every catch" rule has to be re-applied by every person who writes a coroutine, and its absence is invisible in review — the code looks fine, it just never cancels.

Worth separating two questions that this error conflates: which JDK compiles, and which bytecode level ships. Deleting the `jvmTarget` line assumes the answer to the second is "21". Sometimes it legitimately is not — a library consumed by services still on 17, an Android module, a plugin for a host that runs an older JVM. If the 17 was deliberate rather than leftover, the fix is not to delete it but to make **both** compilers agree on 17 while still building with JDK 21:

```
java {
    toolchain { languageVersion = JavaLanguageVersion.of(21) }
}

tasks.withType<JavaCompile>().configureEach {
    options.release.set(17)
}

kotlin {
    compilerOptions {
        jvmTarget.set(org.jetbrains.kotlin.gradle.dsl.JvmTarget.JVM_17)
        freeCompilerArgs.add("-Xjdk-release=17")
    }
}
```

The pair of flags on each side is the part worth understanding.

`options.release` is strictly stronger than `targetCompatibility`. `targetCompatibility = 17` only sets the bytecode version — you can still call a Java 21 API, and the class file loads fine on a 17 JVM right up until it hits that call and throws `NoSuchMethodError`. `--release 17` additionally restricts the visible *API* to what Java 17 exposed, so the mistake becomes a compile error.

Kotlin's `jvmTarget` is the `targetCompatibility` equivalent: bytecode version only. `-Xjdk-release=17` is the `--release` equivalent, restricting the JDK API surface Kotlin code can see. Without it you get the same trap on the Kotlin side — code that compiles on your JDK 21 toolchain and fails at runtime on 17.

Set them and the consistency check passes because both tasks report 17, while the compilers themselves still run on JDK 21.

One last thing to verify once this builds: the `test` task runs on the toolchain JDK (21), not on 17. Your tests therefore do not exercise the runtime you ship to. If that matters, add a second test task with `javaLauncher` pointed at a 17 toolchain — otherwise the whole `--release` apparatus guards compile time only, and nothing checks that the code actually runs there.

The static async factory is the supported pattern, and it is worth taking because it removes the failure mode from the type system rather than guarding against it.

```
class Api {
  final HttpClient _client;

  Api._(this._client);

  static Future<Api> create() async {
    await _loadCerts();
    return Api._(HttpClient());
  }

  Future<String> get(String path) async => _read(_client, path);
}
```

Callers become:

```
final api = await Api.create();
final body = await api.get('/health');
```

The field is `final`, not `late`, so it is initialized before the constructor completes and the compiler proves it. There is no runtime check to fail and no `LateInitializationError` to be had — not "unlikely", but unrepresentable.

The private constructor matters: `Api._(this._client)` makes `Api()` inaccessible from outside the library, so nobody can construct a half-built instance by accident. Without it, the old broken path stays available alongside the new one.

The cost is real and worth stating plainly: every construction site becomes `await`. That propagates up to whoever creates the `Api`, and if that is a widget or a DI registration, it has to become async too. But that is the dependency being honest — the object genuinely cannot serve a request before the certs are loaded, and a synchronous constructor was always a claim that it could.

If the awkwardness lands in a place that cannot be async (a `late final` field on a widget, a service locator registration), do the awaiting once at app startup and register the resolved instance:

```
void main() async {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();
  final api = await Api.create();
  runApp(App(api: api));
}
```

That is the shape to prefer when there is one of these objects with an application lifetime, which is usually the case for an HTTP client.

If you cannot make callers await construction — a service locator, a singleton, a field on a widget — store the `Future` and await it in each method. Dart has a concise form for this:

```
class Api {
  late final Future<HttpClient> _client = _init();

  Future<HttpClient> _init() async {
    await _loadCerts();
    return HttpClient();
  }

  Future<String> get(String path) async => _read(await _client, path);
}
```

Three things make this work where your original did not.

**`late final x = expr` is lazy.** The initializer runs the first time the field is *read*, not in the constructor. So the cert load starts when someone actually calls `get()`, and `Api()` stays a cheap synchronous constructor.

**It runs exactly once.** `late final` with an initializer evaluates it once and caches the result — here, one `Future`. Ten concurrent callers all `await` the same `Future` and all see the same client. No lock, no double-initialization, no race.

**The `await` is inside the method, not at the call site.** `get()` was already `Future<String>`, so nothing about the public API changed.

If you want the work to start eagerly instead, assign in the constructor body — same single shared Future, just kicked off earlier:

```
class Api {
  late final Future<HttpClient> _clientFuture;
  Api() { _clientFuture = _init(); }
}
```

Two limitations to know. Every call pays an `await` even long after warm-up; it is a completed Future so the cost is a microtask, not I/O, but it is not free in a hot loop. And a `Future` that completed with an error keeps that error forever — if `_loadCerts()` fails once, every subsequent call fails identically with no retry. If the setup can fail transiently, you need to clear the field and rebuild, which `late final` will not let you do; use a nullable `Future<HttpClient>?` and reset it in a `catch`.

The deeper fix is to not let `dynamic` past the parsing layer at all. Both `cast` and `from` produce a `TypeError` when the payload disagrees with your assumption, and a `TypeError` is a programming-error signal — it says "the code is wrong", when what actually happened is "the server sent something else". Those want different handling: one is a crash report, the other is a retry or a user-facing message.

Parse into a real type and raise a domain error:

```
class Post {
  final int id;
  final List<String> tags;

  const Post({required this.id, required this.tags});

  factory Post.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> json) {
    final id = json['id'];
    if (id is! int) {
      throw FormatException('Post.id must be an int, got: $id');
    }

    final rawTags = json['tags'] ?? const [];
    if (rawTags is! List) {
      throw FormatException('Post.tags must be a list, got: $rawTags');
    }

    return Post(
      id: id,
      tags: rawTags.map((e) {
        if (e is! String) {
          throw FormatException('Post.tags entry must be a String, got: $e');
        }
        return e;
      }).toList(growable: false),
    );
  }
}
```

Verbose, and worth it at exactly one layer. Above it, `Post` is a type you can trust; below it, every failure is a `FormatException` you can catch around the whole decode and turn into "the server returned something we do not understand" rather than a crash.

Two edge cases the `cast`/`from` discussion does not cover, and which this shape handles:

**Empty lists never fail.** `{"tags": []}` decodes to a `List<dynamic>` with no elements, so `cast` and `from` both succeed and every test passes. The first time a non-empty response arrives with the wrong element type, it breaks in production. Element checks only run when there are elements — your tests need a populated fixture.

**`null` versus missing.** `{"tags": null}` and a payload with no `tags` key at all both give you `null` from the map lookup, and `as List` throws on both. `json['tags'] ?? const []` above treats them as an empty list; if absence and null-valued should differ, use `json.containsKey('tags')` to tell them apart.

If the boilerplate becomes a burden across many models, that is the point at which `json_serializable` or `freezed` pays for itself — they generate this same eager, element-checked parsing from annotations.

The rule is about the *kind* of function, not about what it captures.

`Isolate.spawn` requires its entry point to be a top-level function or a static method. A closure is rejected regardless of whether it captured anything, which matches what you observed. The reason is that the entry point has to be identified to the new isolate by a stable, statically-known reference into the program's code. A top-level or static function has one. A closure is a heap object — code pointer plus a context — created at run time, and the isolate message protocol's set of sendable types does not include closures, so there is nothing to name it by. "Captures nothing" is not a property the sender checks; the object is still a closure.

The corollary catches people: a **static** method works, an **instance** method tear-off does not, because a tear-off implicitly captures the receiver and is therefore a closure.

```
class Parser {
  static void entry(SendPort port) { … }   // OK as an entry point
  void method(SendPort port) { … }         // tear-off is a closure, rejected
}
```

The working shape, which you already found, spelled out with the reply path:

```
import 'dart:isolate';

void _parse(SendPort replyTo) {
  final result = expensiveParse();
  replyTo.send(result);
}

Future<Result> parseInBackground() async {
  final rp = ReceivePort();
  await Isolate.spawn(_parse, rp.sendPort);
  final result = await rp.first as Result;
  rp.close();
  return result;
}
```

Note `rp.close()` — a `ReceivePort` left open keeps the isolate's `main` alive, which is a common leak in this pattern.

If you need to pass more than a port, send a record or a small class holding both: the entry point takes exactly one argument, so `Isolate.spawn(_parse, (rp.sendPort, bytes))` and destructure inside. Everything in that message must itself be sendable, which is the next rule to know.

`Isolate.run` accepts a closure because it does not send your closure as an isolate *message*. It spawns with its own internal entry point and arranges for the computation to run there, then returns the result and shuts the isolate down. From your side it is one call:

```
final result = await Isolate.run(() => expensiveParse(bytes));
```

What it relaxes is the top-level-function requirement. What it does **not** relax is sendability of what the closure captures. Captured values are transferred to the new isolate, so a closure capturing an open socket, a `ReceivePort`, a database handle, or anything transitively holding one fails at run time with the same "Illegal argument in isolate message" — just naming the captured object instead of the closure. Keep captures to plain data and you are fine.

Which brings up the more important problem with your original code, independent of the closure rule:

```
final log = StringBuffer();
await Isolate.spawn((int n) {
  log.write(n);
}, 42);
```

Even if this were accepted, it would not do what you want. Isolates do not share mutable memory. The `StringBuffer` would be **copied** into the new isolate, the write would land in the copy, and `log` in `main` would stay empty. The same is true under `Isolate.run`. There is no "capture by reference" across an isolate boundary — that is the entire point of the model, and it is why the restriction on closures is not merely a limitation but a signal that the code is assuming shared state.

So the two rules together:

- Anything the isolate needs as *input* must be sendable and is copied in.
- Anything it produces must come back through a `SendPort`, or as the return value of `Isolate.run`.

`Isolate.run` is the right default for exactly your case — a one-shot CPU-bound computation with a value in and a value out. Reach for `Isolate.spawn` when the isolate is long-lived and you need a bidirectional channel, which is when the port plumbing is actually buying you something.

Two things worth knowing once you are past the closure rule, because they are what you will hit next.

**Copying is not free, and there is a way around it.** Every message crossing an isolate boundary is deep-copied. For a parse that returns a large structure, the copy back can cost more than the parse you moved off the main thread — people move work to an isolate, measure no improvement, and conclude isolates do not help. Two mitigations:

`Isolate.exit` hands the final message over *without* copying, because the sending isolate is terminating and its memory can be transferred rather than duplicated:

```
void _parse((SendPort, Uint8List) args) {
  final (port, bytes) = args;
  Isolate.exit(port, expensiveParse(bytes));   // no copy
}
```

`TransferableTypedData` does the same for byte buffers going *in*, moving ownership instead of copying:

```
final t = TransferableTypedData.fromList([bytes]);
// in the isolate: final bytes = t.materialize().asUint8List();
```

`Isolate.run` already uses the `Isolate.exit` mechanism for its result, which is another reason to prefer it for one-shot work.

**What is and is not sendable.** Rejected: closures (via `spawn`'s entry point), `ReceivePort` (only `SendPort` is sendable), and anything holding a native resource — sockets, files, `Pointer`s, and any object that transitively references one. That last clause is what bites: a plain-looking DTO fails because it holds a logger that holds a sink. Accepted: primitives, `String`, `List`/`Map`/`Set` of sendable values, typed data, `SendPort`, `Capability`, deeply immutable objects, and — since Dart 3 — ordinary class instances, provided none of their fields is unsendable. So you rarely need to flatten your models into maps any more; you just need to check what they are holding.

When something fails, the message names the specific object, not the field path. The fastest way to find it is to construct the payload explicitly right before the send and comment out fields until it passes — a stack trace will not tell you which reference made it unsendable.

`mounted` is the correct guard and you should keep it, but it is a guard on `setState`, not on the request. Worth being precise about what it is.

`State.mounted` is `_element != null`. The framework sets that when the `State` is unmounted, and `setState` asserts on it because marking a defunct element dirty cannot do anything useful. Your guard is exactly the sanctioned fix:

```
Future<void> _load() async {
  final data = await api.fetch();
  if (!mounted) return;
  setState(() => _items = data);
}
```

Three details that people get wrong with it:

**It must come after each `await`, not once at the top.** A check before the first suspension proves nothing about the state after it. If `_load` has two awaits, it needs two checks.

**`State.mounted` and `BuildContext.mounted` are different things.** `if (mounted)` makes `setState` safe. It does *not* make a captured `context` safe to use — `Navigator.of(context)`, `ScaffoldMessenger.of(context)`, `Theme.of(context)` after an await want `if (context.mounted)`, and the `use_build_context_synchronously` lint checks specifically for that. In a `State` the two usually coincide, but in a helper function taking a `BuildContext` they do not, and that is where the subtle version of this bug lives.

**Unmounted is not always permanent.** A `State` can be deactivated and reactivated within the same frame when a widget with a `GlobalKey` is moved in the tree. Do not build "cancel forever" logic on top of a single `mounted == false` observation.

What it does not do is stop the fetch. The request completes, the bytes arrive, the result is discarded. For a screen the user left, that is usually acceptable — the other answers cover the cases where it is not.

On "is there a standard way to cancel a `Future`": no, and the reason is structural. A `Future` is a handle to a result, not a handle to the work. `await`ing is not subscribing — there is nothing to unsubscribe from, and no hook the framework could call. Cancellation has to be implemented by whatever is actually doing the I/O.

For `package:http`, close the client:

```
class _FeedPageState extends State<FeedPage> {
  final _client = http.Client();

  Future<void> _load() async {
    try {
      final res = await _client.get(uri);
      if (!mounted) return;
      setState(() => _items = parse(res.body));
    } on http.ClientException {
      // client was closed, or the request genuinely failed
    }
  }

  @override
  void dispose() {
    _client.close();
    super.dispose();
  }
}
```

`close()` tears down in-flight requests on the IO client, so the pending `Future` completes with an error rather than a value — hence the `catch`. This is the real thing: the socket goes away and the server stops sending.

Other clients have their own mechanism. Dio uses `CancelToken` (`dio.get(url, cancelToken: token)` and `token.cancel()` in `dispose`). A raw `HttpClient` gives you `HttpClientRequest.abort()`. `StreamSubscription` has `cancel()`.

If you only need to stop *your callbacks* running — not the underlying work — `CancelableOperation` from `package:async` is the standard wrapper:

```
CancelableOperation<List<Item>>? _op;

void _load() {
  _op = CancelableOperation.fromFuture(api.fetch());
  _op!.value.then((data) {
    if (mounted) setState(() => _items = data);
  });
}

@override
void dispose() {
  _op?.cancel();
  super.dispose();
}
```

Be clear on what that buys: `cancel()` prevents the `then` from firing. It does not abort the HTTP request unless you also pass an `onCancel` that does so. It is a cleaner `mounted` check, not cancellation.

For the places where you need the result — "to write it to a cache" — the conclusion to draw is that the request does not belong to the widget.

A widget's lifetime is "while this is on screen". A fetch-and-cache has a different lifetime: it should finish whether or not the user stayed. Tying it to a `State` means you are choosing between two bad options — cancel it (and lose the cache write) or let it complete (and guard every touch of the widget).

Move the work up to something that outlives the screen:

```
class FeedRepository {
  Future<List<Item>> load() async {
    final data = await api.fetch();
    await cache.write(data);       // happens regardless of navigation
    return data;
  }
}
```

The widget then only observes. The lifecycle problem disappears because there is no `setState` in an async callback to guard:

```
late final Future<List<Item>> _future = repo.load();

@override
Widget build(BuildContext context) {
  return FutureBuilder<List<Item>>(
    future: _future,
    builder: (context, snapshot) {
      if (snapshot.connectionState != ConnectionState.done) {
        return const CircularProgressIndicator();
      }
      if (snapshot.hasError) return ErrorView(snapshot.error!);
      return FeedList(items: snapshot.data!);
    },
  );
}
```

`FutureBuilder` does not cancel anything either — the repository call still completes and still writes the cache, which is what you wanted — but the framework owns the subscription and drops it on dispose, so there is no defunct-`setState` to be had.

The mistake to avoid here is calling `repo.load()` inside `build()`. `build` runs on every rebuild — a parent rebuild, a keyboard opening, a theme change — and each call starts a new request. Create the `Future` once, in `initState` or as a `late final` field as above.

Same reasoning applies with any state-management layer: a Bloc, a `ChangeNotifier` held above the route, a Riverpod provider. The common property is that the object owning the request outlives the widget observing it, which is what "I need the result even if the user left" actually requires.

One correction to the premise, because it changes how urgent this is.

`setState() called after dispose()` comes from an assertion. Assertions are enabled in debug and profile builds and **compiled out in release**. In a release build the call is a silent no-op: `setState` marks a defunct element dirty, nothing rebuilds, nothing crashes. So this is not a production stability problem, and the red console output overstates its severity.

That matters because it tells you what the message is really for. It is not "your app will crash" — it is "an async operation outlived the widget that started it". Adding `if (mounted)` silences the messenger. Whether that is the right response depends on what the async operation is:

- A read-only fetch whose result only feeds the UI: `mounted` is genuinely the whole answer. The wasted request is a rounding error.
- A fetch with a side effect (cache write, analytics, a lock to release): the guard silently drops the side effect. That is a real bug, now invisible.
- A repeated operation on a screen users enter and leave quickly: each visit leaves a request in flight. `mounted` makes the symptom go away while the resource usage grows.

Before you conclude the guard is sufficient, check which one you have. Cheap instrumentation:

```
int _inFlight = 0;

Future<void> _load() async {
  _inFlight++;
  debugPrint('in flight: $_inFlight');
  try {
    final data = await api.fetch();
    if (!mounted) return;
    setState(() => _items = data);
  } finally {
    _inFlight--;
  }
}
```

Push and pop the route a dozen times. If the count returns to zero promptly, the guard is fine. If it climbs, you have a resource problem that `mounted` was hiding, and the fix is cancellation or moving the request out of the widget.

The lint (`use_build_context_synchronously`) and the analyzer suggestion push you toward the guard because it is right most of the time. Just do not read "the error is gone" as "the async lifetime is now correct" — those are different claims.

Worth stepping back: keys fix reconciliation, but per-row `State` in a scrolling list has a second failure mode that keys cannot fix, and you will hit it as soon as the list grows.

`ListView(children: [...])` builds every child eagerly. That is fine for three rows and wrong past a few dozen. The moment you switch to the lazy constructor — which you should — off-screen rows are *disposed*, and their `State` goes with them:

```
ListView.builder(
  itemCount: _items.length,
  itemBuilder: (context, i) => CounterTile(
    key: ValueKey(_items[i].id),
    label: _items[i].label,
  ),
)
```

Scroll a counter off screen, scroll back, and the count is gone. Not a key problem — the state no longer exists to be matched. `AutomaticKeepAliveClientMixin` can prevent the disposal, but keeping every row alive defeats the point of the lazy builder and reintroduces the memory cost you switched away from.

The durable fix is to move the count out of the row:

```
class Item {
  final String id;
  final String label;
  final int count;
  const Item({required this.id, required this.label, this.count = 0});

  Item copyWith({int? count}) =>
      Item(id: id, label: label, count: count ?? this.count);
}
```

`CounterTile` becomes stateless, takes `count` and an `onIncrement` callback, and the list owner holds the data:

```
CounterTile(
  key: ValueKey(item.id),
  label: item.label,
  count: item.count,
  onIncrement: () => setState(() {
    _items[i] = _items[i].copyWith(count: _items[i].count + 1);
  }),
)
```

Now reordering, removal, scrolling, hot reload and process death all behave, because the data lives where the list lives. The keys remain worth having — they still let Flutter reuse elements instead of rebuilding subtrees — but they stop being load-bearing for correctness.

The trade-off is honest: a callback per row and a copy-on-write model instead of a local `int++`. What you buy is that the state is somewhere you can serialise, test, and reason about, rather than distributed across `Element` objects whose lifetimes the scroll position controls.

One more thing to check once the key is in, because it produces a bug that looks like the key did not work.

Adding `key: ValueKey(item.id)` means an element is reused only when the id matches. It does **not** mean the widget is unchanged. When `CounterTile` is rebuilt with the same key and a different `label`, the framework reuses the `State` and calls `didUpdateWidget`. If your `State` copied the label into a field in `initState`, that copy is now stale:

```
class _CounterTileState extends State<CounterTile> {
  late String _label;
  int _count = 0;

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    _label = widget.label;   // runs once — will not see later changes
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) => Text('$_label: $_count');
}
```

Rename an item and the row keeps the old text, because `initState` does not run again. Two fixes, and the first is almost always the right one:

```
// Read from the widget in build; nothing to keep in sync.
Widget build(BuildContext context) => Text('${widget.label}: $_count');
```

```
// Or, if the value must be transformed or drives a controller:
@override
void didUpdateWidget(CounterTile old) {
  super.didUpdateWidget(old);
  if (old.label != widget.label) _label = widget.label;
}
```

The rule of thumb: a `State` should hold only what is genuinely local and cannot be derived from the widget. `_count` qualifies; `_label` does not. Everything derived from `widget` should be read in `build`, or explicitly reconciled in `didUpdateWidget`.

This is the same principle as the key itself, from the other direction. The key controls *which* `State` a widget is paired with; `didUpdateWidget` controls what that `State` does when its configuration changes. Getting the key right and then caching configuration in `initState` gives you rows whose identity is correct and whose contents are stale — which is harder to spot than the original bug, because it only shows when the data changes rather than when the list does.

The same trap applies to `TextEditingController`, `AnimationController` and `ScrollController` created in `initState` from widget values.

If the token check says you are on a background isolate, the fix is explicit and has been supported since Flutter 3.7.

A spawned isolate gets no `BinaryMessenger`, so every method channel call has nothing to dispatch to and throws `MissingPluginException`. You have to hand it the root isolate's token and initialise the messenger before the first plugin call:

```
import 'dart:isolate';
import 'dart:ui';
import 'package:flutter/services.dart';
import 'package:shared_preferences/shared_preferences.dart';

// root isolate
Future<void> startTask() async {
  final token = RootIsolateToken.instance!;
  await Isolate.spawn(_taskEntry, token);
}

// background isolate
Future<void> _taskEntry(RootIsolateToken token) async {
  BackgroundIsolateBinaryMessenger.ensureInitialized(token);

  final prefs = await SharedPreferences.getInstance();
  final value = prefs.getString('lastSync');
  // ...
}
```

`ensureInitialized` must run before any plugin call in that isolate, and the token has to be captured on the root isolate — `RootIsolateToken.instance` is null in the background one, so you cannot fetch it there.

Three caveats worth having up front:

**This wires the messenger, not the plugin's capabilities.** A plugin whose Android implementation needs an `Activity` rather than just a `Context` still will not work from a background isolate. `shared_preferences` is Context-only, so it is fine.

**If the isolate is spawned by a plugin** — `workmanager`, `android_alarm_manager_plus`, a `flutter_local_notifications` background handler — that plugin has its own entry-point contract, usually a `@pragma('vm:entry-point')` top-level function, and it calls `DartPluginRegistrant.ensureInitialized()` for you. Read its docs before adding `BackgroundIsolateBinaryMessenger` on top; doing both is not harmful but doing neither is the usual bug.

**The two isolates do not share the preferences cache.** `SharedPreferences` keeps a Dart-side in-memory copy per isolate. A write in the background isolate is not visible to an already-loaded instance in the root isolate until you call `reload()`, and vice versa. If both sides write the same key, one will silently overwrite the other's view.

A cause neither of your two theories covers, and it fits "Android but not iOS" better than either: calling a plugin before the binding is initialised.

`MissingPluginException` also occurs when the plugin *is* registered but the call happens before the engine's messenger is attached to the Dart side. The classic shape is a plugin call in a field initialiser or in `main()` before `ensureInitialized`:

```
// wrong — runs before the binding exists
final prefsFuture = SharedPreferences.getInstance();

void main() {
  runApp(const App());
}
```

```
// right
void main() async {
  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();
  final prefs = await SharedPreferences.getInstance();
  runApp(App(prefs: prefs));
}
```

Top-level variables in Dart are lazy, so this only bites when something reads `prefsFuture` early — which is exactly the kind of timing a hot restart changes, because the engine is already warm and the Dart side comes up faster than on a cold start. That is a plausible mechanism for "works on `flutter run`, fails on hot restart" that has nothing to do with native registration.

It also explains the iOS/Android asymmetry. The two platforms attach the engine at different points in their startup, so the same race can be lost on one and won on the other. Platform-specific timing differences are usually a sign of a race, not of a missing implementation.

How to distinguish it from the registration theories: registration problems fail *every* call, forever, until the app is restarted. A binding-initialisation race fails only the earliest calls and succeeds later. Try the same preference read from a button press after the UI is up:

```
ElevatedButton(
  onPressed: () async {
    final prefs = await SharedPreferences.getInstance();
    debugPrint('late read ok: ${prefs.getKeys()}');
  },
  child: const Text('probe'),
)
```

If the button works after a hot restart while the startup call throws, it is timing. If the button throws too, registration or the isolate is the cause and you can go back to the other answers.
